<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        HOW THE PANEL ABOUT ONE ENTRY LOOKS - its surface, its four kinds of text, and the marks it
        borrows from the list. Seventeenth theme file, 2026-09-16, and it exists because of a
        measurement rather than a preference: the owner said the panel was black text on grey, and
        tools/gui-probe/details-shot.ps1 read the pixels and found not one pixel lighter than the
        ground anywhere a value was drawn. The values WERE black - #000000 on #202020, a ratio of
        1.29 - for the thirty-four days since the panel was built, because the only style they had
        was declared in the view, based on the implicit TextBlock style, and named no colour. The
        guard that catches exactly that reads this directory and not the views, which is why the
        styles moved here: a colour named in a theme file is a colour something checks.

        ADR-23, the same rule as every other theme file: not one value of its own, every number and
        colour below is a name declared in Values.xaml, Spacing.xaml, Type.xaml or Colours.xaml.

        MERGED AFTER Marks.xaml AND BEFORE Catalogue.xaml, and the position is load bearing in the
        ordinary way: three styles below extend the list's marks by StaticResource, which resolves
        while this file is being read. App.xaml, the test host and AppearanceGuards all carry that
        order, and CatalogueGuards holds App.xaml and the host to the same list.

        THE DESIGN THIS DRAWS IS docs/PROJEKT-SZCZEGOLY-20260916.md, and the reasoning that is
        not repeated here lives there - four levels of hierarchy on one axis each (size for the
        title, weight for a section, colour for a value against its label), a surface so the panel
        is not the same #202020 the list stands on, and the list's own marks beside the status and
        the start type so one entry is not drawn in two languages.
    -->

    <!--
        THE SURFACE. What the plan sheet has (PlanSheet in Plan.xaml) without the corner and the
        margin: this panel is docked beside the list rather than floating over it - docs/11 3.12,
        an inspector shows and a sheet asks - so it takes the sheet's fill and its one-point edge on
        the side facing the list, and nothing that says "above".

        WHAT THE FILL COSTS, said here because ContrastGuards will not say it: every ratio that
        guard measures is against the WINDOW, and #343434 is lighter, so every colour on this panel
        reads lower than the number in Colours.xaml. TextSubdued is 4.76 here against 6.23 there,
        measured with tools/contrast/contrast.ps1 -Background '#343434' on 2026-09-16. Over the
        4.5 that WCAG 1.4.3 asks, and the reason the labels are thirteen points rather than twelve.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="DetailPanel" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfacePanel}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource SurfacePanelEdge}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{StaticResource BorderPanelEdge}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PaddingPanel}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE PANEL'S TITLE IS THE DISPLAY NAME, AND IT MAY TAKE TWO LINES. HeadingText as before,
        plus wrapping under a ceiling of two lines with an ellipsis on the last: the owner's own
        capture shows the Bluetooth user service's Polish display name - forty-five characters -
        cut after four words on one line, in a panel whose whole purpose is to show the entry in
        full. HeightDetailTitle is two lines of TextSizeHeading and
        is held to that by TypeScaleGuards, so the ceiling cannot drift from the size it was made
        for. The whole name is still in the tooltip, as every cell in the list keeps it.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="DetailTitleText" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource HeadingText}">
        <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
        <Setter Property="TextTrimming" Value="CharacterEllipsis" />
        <Setter Property="MaxHeight" Value="{StaticResource HeightDetailTitle}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE HEADING OF ONE SECTION - FOURTEEN, SEMIBOLD, WHITE - AND THAT IS THE ONE PLACE THIS
        PANEL DEPARTS FROM THE PLAN SHEET ON PURPOSE. Fourteen and not the body's thirteen because
        TypeScaleGuards holds semibold text to the fourteen-point floor of docs/11 3.6, and said so
        on the first build - the ladder's Body Strong, the size PlanTitleText already uses. The sheet's SectionHeadingText is thirteen in
        TextSubdued, and Text.xaml carries the owner's reason: under those headings stand white
        sentences, so a grey heading is the only grey thing in its block and position finds it.
        Under THESE headings stands a form whose whole left column is grey - a grey heading would
        be the twenty-ninth label. Weight is what a label does not have, so weight is what says
        "this is a section"; the rule down the block's left side (PanelBlock) says where it ends.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="DetailSectionText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginBelowSearch}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        A SENTENCE ABOUT A WHOLE SECTION, shown only when there is one - today that is the note
        saying that a field marked "not read" is read once its column is on. It stands UNDER the
        lines, with the gap above it that a status line takes, because the lines it explains are
        the ones above it. Collapsed on the empty string, so a section with nothing to say spends
        no line on saying it.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="DetailNoteText" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource SubduedText}">
        <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginAboveStatus}" />
        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Note}" Value="">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        WHAT A FIELD IS CALLED. TextSubdued at body size - thirteen, not the twelve of SubduedText,
        because the label and the value beside it differ on COLOUR and nothing else; a second
        difference in size made the label the smallest and dimmest thing on the panel, which is
        half of what the owner meant by "cannot be seen". BodySubduedText since 2026-09-16, which
        is exactly that, said once in Text.xaml rather than a fourth time here.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="DetailLabelText" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource BodySubduedText}">
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Top" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        WHAT THIS ENTRY SAYS IN IT - white, because it is the answer. Wraps rather than trims: this
        panel is where somebody comes to READ a description of 1251 characters, and the cell it
        came from is the place that trims.

        THE FIXED FACE FOR A PATH, A HASH AND A DESCRIPTOR, decided by the line's Wears code rather
        than by which column it is - docs/03 part 4, alignment carries meaning where two different
        values must not be able to look the same. And the subdued colour for a value that is not
        one: "none", "not read" and "no access" are states of the reading, and a state wears the
        colour of the label so it cannot be mistaken for something the machine said. Built on
        BodyText since 2026-09-16: the white comes from there, and the colour guard follows
        BasedOn to find it - the day before, a style built on another was invisible to it.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="DetailValueText" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource BodyText}">
        <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Wears}" Value="fixed">
                <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFaceFixed}" />
            </DataTrigger>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Missing}" Value="True">
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE LIST'S OWN MARKS, EACH SHOWN ONLY ON THE LINE THAT WEARS IT. The base styles in
        Marks.xaml decide colour and shape from a code named StatusShape, StartShape or
        AgainstShape on whatever they are bound to - a row in the list, a line here - so the
        panel cannot draw a running entry in a colour the list does not use. What this file adds is
        only WHEN: collapsed unless the line's Wears code is this mark's, so a line about the
        account carries no dot and the mark column is one width shared by every line.

        THE START AND THE MISMATCH MARK HAVE Hidden IN THEIR BASE AND Visible IN THEIR BASE'S
        TRIGGERS. A trigger in the base outranks a setter in the derived style, so on the one line
        that wears them their own code turns them on exactly as it does in the list, and on every
        other line the code is empty, no trigger matches, and Collapsed from below holds. Measured
        on the screen on 2026-09-16, recorded in docs/PROJEKT-SZCZEGOLY-20260916.md section 10.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="DetailStatusMark" TargetType="Ellipse" BasedOn="{StaticResource StatusMark}">
        <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Top" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginDetailMark}" />
        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Wears}" Value="status">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="DetailStartMark" TargetType="Ellipse" BasedOn="{StaticResource StartMark}">
        <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Top" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginDetailMark}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="DetailAgainstMark" TargetType="Path" BasedOn="{StaticResource AgainstMark}">
        <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Top" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginDetailMark}" />
    </Style>
</ResourceDictionary>
